Kazi_38_1_2527                  Kazi_38_1_2527                  Hurry! The fiend awakens...
Ventrue_39_1_1464               Ventrue_39_1_1464               Hey, where's those guys with the painting?
Ventrue_39_1_1465               Ventrue_39_1_1465               Yeah. Where are they? Maybe they got stuck in traffic.
Ventrue_39_1_1466               Ventrue_39_1_1466               They better hurry up. It's almost V-day.
Ventrue_39_1_1467               Ventrue_39_1_1467               Yeah. V-day.
Christof_39_1_2499              Christof_39_1_2499              Speak now, or forfeit the pleasure of speech forever!
Anezka_0_0_1973                 Anezka_0_0_1973                 O sir, I pray that the light of the Lord fill thy body with strength and vigor.
Anezka_0_0_111                  Anezka_0_0_111                  Oh sir, I pray for thy safety on so perilous a journey!
DABart1_4_1_2512                DABart1_4_1_2512                Hail good Sir Knight, and welcome. They do say you seek to cleanse the mines of evil. Oh, these demons have brought great suffering to the good people of Prague. Our craftsmen have no silver for their wares, and many proud families now beg for their bread. Please accept all that my humble inn can provide, and the blessings of the Lord be upon your quest.
StJohn3_4_1_2505                StJohn3_4_1_2505                Beware the mines, Crusader. If mere men could kill the hell-spawn, we Knights of St. John would have slain them long ago.
StJohn4_4_1_2506                StJohn4_4_1_2506                We lock these gates as the last rays of the sun die in the mountains. Pass within before dark, or wait without until dawn. I pray for thy safe return.
Geza_0_0_110                    Geza_0_0_110                    God save and protect thee, his soldier, his wrath and vengeance.
Geza_0_0_1972                   Geza_0_0_1972                   Why dost thou tarry on thy sacred mission? Get thee hence unto places of evil!
StJohn1_4_4_99                  StJohn1_4_4_99                  Hail and well met, Crusader.
Christof_4_4_100                Christof_4_4_100                Hail and well met to thee, Knights of Saint John.
StJohn1_4_4_97                  StJohn1_4_4_97                  'Tis good to hear the voice of a fellow Frenchman. We Knights of St. John are far from home, and I would I were back in France.
Christof_4_4_98                 Christof_4_4_98                 Say not so. We should gladly go to the ends of the Earth in service to our lord. Good day to thee.
Christof_4_1_52                 Christof_4_1_52                 The cheerful daylight world belies the horrors that prowl by night. How can the good people of Prague go about their business with such evil abroad in the land?
Christof_4_3_87                 Christof_4_3_87                 The shop of a gypsy witch. Dare I enter such a place?
Christof_4_2_53                 Christof_4_2_53                 Ah, a smithy. Now I may replace my war-weary armor.
Christof_4_2_54                 Christof_4_2_54                 Hail, good sir blacksmith. I am Christof, of the Swordbrethren, and I seek to replace armor lost in battle.
Jiri_4_2_55                     Jiri_4_2_55                     Ho there, young Crusader. I be Jiri Borijov, and the very boards of me humble shop are honored to be trodden by a Swordbrethren. And who will ye be slayin' in yer new armor?
Christof_4_2_56                 Christof_4_2_56                 Such things are beyond the concern of a smithy.
Christof_4_2_57                 Christof_4_2_57                 I seek to slay the demons in the silver mines to the east.
Jiri_4_2_58                     Jiri_4_2_58                     Beggin' yer pardon, sir. I mean no offense.
Jiri_4_2_63                     Jiri_4_2_63                     Ye look a bit sickly, Sir Christof. If ye do seek to spill the blood of the fiends in the mine, ye'll be needing... special protection.
Christof_4_2_64                 Christof_4_2_64                 The protection of the Lord suffices. I need no more than that.
Jiri_4_2_65                     Jiri_4_2_65                     Ha! Tell that to yer armor and sword! Nay, 'tis Gypsy charms I be speakin' of.
Christof_4_2_66                 Christof_4_2_66                 Aid from a gypsy? Can such a thing be godly?
Christof_4_2_67                 Christof_4_2_67                 Never! Such magicks are of the devil.
Christof_4_2_68                 Christof_4_2_68                 Where shall I find such gypsy charms?
Jiri_4_2_69                     Jiri_4_2_69                     Why, did not King Saul seek out a wise woman and her familiar spirit in his time of need? Yea, that very King Saul who had once banished all witches, sought a witch when he needed to learn of his future. She summoned the spirit of Samuel the Prophet, and no mistake. 'Tis in the holy Bible!
Christof_4_2_70                 Christof_4_2_70                 I do not wish to risk my soul with reckless alliances.
Christof_4_2_71                 Christof_4_2_71                 There is wisdom in thy words. I will go to this gypsy.
Jiri_4_2_72                     Jiri_4_2_72                     Yer zeal in the name of our Lord is commendable. But take care, lest such stubborn pride doom yer soul. Some do say the gypsy is evil, but I say nay. I do know the healing power of the Lord, and her potions do restore my spirit as well as my limbs.
Christof_4_2_73                 Christof_4_2_73                 Very well. Tell me of her and I shall seek her out.
Christof_4_2_74                 Christof_4_2_74                 I entrust not the care of my limbs to gypsies, nor the care of my soul to blacksmiths.
Jiri_4_2_75                     Jiri_4_2_75                     I do beg yer pardon, milord. I meant no ill. God be with ye.
Jiri_4_2_76                     Jiri_4_2_76                     Ye must promise me that the gypsy will come to no harm by my telling.
Christof_4_2_77                 Christof_4_2_77                 No. I cannot promise that. Harm is bound to come to those doing the devil's work.
Christof_4_2_78                 Christof_4_2_78                 I promise.
Jiri_4_2_79                     Jiri_4_2_79                     Then I can not be tellin'  ye. God be with ye.
Christof_4_2_80                 Christof_4_2_80                 Farewell.
Christof_4_2_81                 Christof_4_2_81                 Very well, Jiri. I promise that no harm shall come to her from thy telling.
Jiri_4_2_82                     Jiri_4_2_82                     Go ye to the Golden Lane, where ye'll find an herbal shop run by the old gypsy woman Unorna. There ye shall find something to aid ye surely. God be with ye, young one.
Stranger_0_0_112                Stranger_0_0_112                Take care outside the gates of the city, good sir knight. For all those that wander the highways alone after nightfall are seldom heard from again. Even the strongest of warriors have disappeared. The streets of Prague are no longer safe after dark, but beyond the city walls, all hell walks the night. Hell that disgorges from yonder castle of Vysehrad!
Unorna_4_3_88                   Unorna_4_3_88                   Hello, young one, what is it you desire?
Christof_4_3_89                 Christof_4_3_89                 I desire only to bring the Lord's justice to the evil that has befallen this city. I would know of the devils that rule the night in Prague.
Unorna_4_3_90                   Unorna_4_3_90                   They are vampires! The undead! The blood drinkers! The fiends do hide their miserable carcasses away in mines and crypts under the earth, for the very rays of the sun do scald the flesh from their bones. 'Tis quite a sight, and a stench the like of which you'll ne'er forget.
Christof_4_3_91                 Christof_4_3_91                 Then perhaps I shall show them the glory of the breaking dawn before dealing them their final mercy.
Unorna_4_3_92                   Unorna_4_3_92                   Weapons canna' kill the fiends, lest their heads be cleft from their undying bodies. You will need more than a buckler of faith to aid you.
Unorna_4_3_93                   Unorna_4_3_93                   Go with God, young one. And be sure to be within the warmth of a hearth when the sun falls, for the foes you have seen are but a small part of the wickedness that walks the streets of Prague by night!
Christof_4_3_94                 Christof_4_3_94                 I shall send all such wickedness back to hell.
Vukodlak_40_4_1547              Vukodlak_40_4_1547              Little fool! You have no hope of winning.
Christof_40_4_1548              Christof_40_4_1548              I have hope beyond hope.
Vukodlak_40_4_1549              Vukodlak_40_4_1549              Aaargh! You waste my time! I have no more patience for you!
Vukodlak_40_4_1550              Vukodlak_40_4_1550              Your skin and bones will add to the walls of my Cathedral of Flesh!
Libussa_40_3_1505               Libussa_40_3_1505               The Master has awoken, death comes to you all!
Libussa_40_5_1590               Libussa_40_5_1590               Go now! There is little time! Destroy him, and topple this vile cathedral!
Libussa_40_5_1591               Libussa_40_5_1591               Now my dark love, I give you thanks for allowing me to serve you all these years, watching over your sweetly cruel visage in torpor. My defilement is complete.
Libussa_40_4_1581               Libussa_40_4_1581               Come with me, quickly! I can help you!
Christof_40_4_1582              Christof_40_4_1582              Why should I enter thy trap, servant of the Fiend?
Libussa_40_4_1583               Libussa_40_4_1583               Please! For Anezka! She says that if you would have hope even when your cause is lost, you should follow me!
Christof_40_4_1585              Christof_40_4_1585              Nay. Thou art defiled and damned. Get thee behind me.
Christof_40_4_1584              Christof_40_4_1584              I go. But I go with my sword at thy back.
Vukodlak_40_4_1506              Vukodlak_40_4_1506              Enter freely, Christof. I bid you welcome to my demesne.
Vukodlak_40_4_1507              Vukodlak_40_4_1507              It is fitting that you should be here now, at the beginning of the New Age, as you were at the end of the last era.
Christof_40_4_1508              Christof_40_4_1508              I shall cause thy fall now as I did then.
Vukodlak_40_4_1509              Vukodlak_40_4_1509              Ah, but then was I not yet arisen. Your untimely intervention set me back nearly a millennium. But I am nothing if not patient. And now I am omnipotent.
Christof_40_4_1510              Christof_40_4_1510              Nay! For we have denied thee Orsi's statue for your ritual!
Vukodlak_40_4_1511              Vukodlak_40_4_1511              I'll live.
Christof_40_4_1512              Christof_40_4_1512              We shall stop thee!
Vukodlak_40_4_1513              Vukodlak_40_4_1513              It's over, Christof. I have already risen, with power enough to consume even Antediluvians.
Christof_40_4_1514              Christof_40_4_1514              No!
Vukodlak_40_4_1515              Vukodlak_40_4_1515              But I must extend my gratitude to you, Christof. My former vessel, Libussa, lacked a certain... purity. But Anezka... ah! Her virtue bubbles up from so rich a spring that I have been endlessly supplied with purity- to defile! I would never have found her without your assistance. You sent her to me. And she has been so loyal and... willing a servant to me over these long eons. I shall now Embrace her, and she will rule with me over the cattle of humanity for all time.
Christof_40_4_1516              Christof_40_4_1516              Nay! Anezka is not thine!
Christof_40_4_1517              Christof_40_4_1517              She serves thee not! She sent a summons to awaken me from Torpor!
Vukodlak_40_4_1518              Vukodlak_40_4_1518              Is this true, Anezka?
Anezka_40_4_1519                Anezka_40_4_1519                Dear Master. Please allow me to split Christof's joints and fleshcraft his bones into a throne for thy glory.
Vukodlak_40_4_1520              Vukodlak_40_4_1520              Ha ha ha ha!
Christof_40_4_1521              Christof_40_4_1521              Monster! I'll destroy thee!
Vukodlak_40_4_1522              Vukodlak_40_4_1522              I have a better idea. Serve me.
Christof_40_4_1523              Christof_40_4_1523              I know thou art wicked, but I never thought thee a fool. .
Vukodlak_40_4_1524              Vukodlak_40_4_1524              Serve me. You don't really have any love for the Antediluvians, do you? The day of Gehenna is coming, and they intend to consume you, and Wilhem, and Lily, and all other vampires. I will prevent that! I will give you eternity! Life everlasting! We will greet the awakening grandsires with another flood, a deluge of their own blood!
Christof_40_4_1525              Christof_40_4_1525              One Antediluvian is no better than thirteen. I cannot entrust such power unto any, let alone thee.
Vukodlak_40_4_1526              Vukodlak_40_4_1526              Then suppose I gave you your beloved Anezka, as well.
Christof_40_4_1527              Christof_40_4_1527              What?
Vukodlak_40_4_1528              Vukodlak_40_4_1528              Consider this. Even if you could destroy me, which you cannot, you would destroy her as well. Remember, she is my Ghoul, and after all these centuries she is utterly dependent on my blood. If I die, she shatters into thousand year-old dust. But serve me, and I'll give her to you. What say you?
Christof_40_4_1529              Christof_40_4_1529              I said I would have her at any cost. I agree to your terms, my master.
Christof_40_4_1530              Christof_40_4_1530              There is another way! I can save Anezka If I consume thy blood, and take all thy ill-gotten power as my own!
Christof_40_4_1531              Christof_40_4_1531              Nay. I shall fight thee to stop thy mad scheme! Even at the cost of my life!
MDVict1_40_4_1551               MDVict1_40_4_1551               I am the Wall of Memory. I contain the secret thoughts of Anezka.
Anezka_40_4_1552                Anezka_40_4_1552                I am damned. In the holy orders I once dreamed of the eternity of bliss that would be mine for my devotion to the Lord. Eternity is now mine, but not bliss. I shall live as long as the Voivode, or as long as the Voivode is pleased with me. Or as long as the Voivode is pleased to defile me. Heavenly bliss is not to be my lot. Only empty torment. Yet still I dare to hope. Still I live.
Christof_40_4_1553              Christof_40_4_1553              Oh, Anezka...
Anezka_40_4_1578                Anezka_40_4_1578                Vukodlak's rage is implacable. His resurrection has failed so often that he blames all the Tzimisce of Europe. He desires to move our entire Cathedral of Flesh to the New World, where we may begin again. This vast undertaking will involve many shipments of native soil. I am well pleased, for this foolish migration shall take at least a century, and further postpone his resurrection.
Anezka_40_4_1579                Anezka_40_4_1579                Alas! I fear I can no longer prevent the Voivode's awakening. Lord Vukodlak has gained great power from the fear and apprehension afoot in the world on the eve of the new millennium. These fears feed him, and despite my greatest efforts, his resurrection is now inescapable. There are none who can help me. I can only hope against hope for the return of the one man Vukodlak fears. I pray that God restores Christof.
Anezka_40_4_1554                Anezka_40_4_1554                I have lived for fifty years. All I loved are dead and gone. My beloved Christof has perished in the fall of Vysehrad. His sacrifice has preserved innocence in the world, and so I rejoice. But my suffering grows with each passing night. The Voivode takes great delight in seeing me rise with fresh hope, for it means he may once again drag me down. Should I surrender all hope, and break, as Libussa is broken, his pleasure would be at an end. Yet I will not abandon my hope, even though such hope fills his empty heart with glee, as it fills mine with pain.
Christof_40_4_1555              Christof_40_4_1555              I cannot bear it...
Anezka_40_4_1563                Anezka_40_4_1563                For more than two hundred years I have suffered every possible form of humiliation and defilement. I have fallen from the chaste and inviolate woman I once was, yet I have borne it all with hope in my heart. But now I am sinking into the murky depths, and the last whisper of hope in me is fading like the final bubbles of breath from a drowning woman. As we grow close to the day of his resurrection, Vukodlak has found the only defilement I cannot bear. He has decreed that I inflict defilement on others by binding innocent men and women into the walls of his accursed Cathedral of Flesh. His power compels me, and I cannot resist a direct command. I can endure my own degradation. But I cannot abide to befoul others. I fear I shall shatter like glass.
Christof_40_4_1557              Christof_40_4_1557              My poor, poor Anezka. This is my doing. Every humiliation she suffers is on my head.
Lily_40_4_1558                  Lily_40_4_1558                  Christof, you can't blame yourself for the choices she made.
Christof_40_4_1559              Christof_40_4_1559              Silence!
Christof_40_4_1560              Christof_40_4_1560              Aye. Perhaps thou art correct.
Lily_40_4_1561                  Lily_40_4_1561                  It's not your fault. You have to move on.
Anezka_40_4_1564                Anezka_40_4_1564                It is two centuries and a half after Vukodlak took me, and his resurrection draws nigh. He now demands that I surrender the final shreds of my honor and perform evil acts of my own volition. I cannot defy my master's command that I defile others, and when I resist him, my poor body carries out his tasks against my will, like a puppet. It is driving me mad. So, I must change. Rather than resist the evil I am compelled to do, I now embrace it, and perform my tasks with zeal. The master favors me, and as I embrace his evil, he gives me greater power. I have been Libussa's equal since my arrival. Now shall I take over her duties, gladly trading the final measure of my innocence for control over the Voivode's worldly affairs. 'Tis the only way.
Lily_40_4_1565                  Lily_40_4_1565                  Oh no! She gave up! I'm so sorry, Christof. Christof? Are you OK?
Christof_40_4_1566              Christof_40_4_1566              I mourn for her soul.
Christof_40_4_1567              Christof_40_4_1567              Thou dost squander precious time in foolish talk. Come. We still have much blood to spill.
Anezka_40_4_1568                Anezka_40_4_1568                I have now lived more than three hundred years past my natural lifespan. I have committed atrocities beyond all imagining. But my plan has succeeded. Though Vukodlak has extraordinary power, his exile from the world makes him weak. He must act through Libussa, who wields great power within our haven. But in just a few years I have usurped all of Libussa's power, and left her a wretched outcast in the Cathedral of Flesh she helped build. Long ago the Voivode stripped her of her soul, and gave her power in exchange. Now that I have taken away that power, she is a pitiable creature indeed. She has no place here, yet she cannot leave. I would lament her fate, but I have no tears to shed, even for myself. For resurrection day draws nigh.
Lily_40_4_1569                  Lily_40_4_1569                  That gives me the creeps. This is supposed to be the purest woman in the world?
Samuel_40_4_1570                Samuel_40_4_1570                Ah, Lily, I think you should really zip it.
Lily_40_4_1571                  Lily_40_4_1571                  I'm only trying to help Christof get over-
Samuel_40_4_1572                Samuel_40_4_1572                Zip it!
Lily_40_4_1573                  Lily_40_4_1573                  You're right. Christof, I'm so sorry. You must be feeling awful. I wish I could...
Christof_40_4_1574              Christof_40_4_1574              My feelings be damned. Tonight we slaughter every denizen of this foul place or die in the attempt.
Anezka_40_4_1575                Anezka_40_4_1575                On the eve of his resurrection, Vukodlak bade us slaughter the Premysl leaders even as they began the ritual that would raise him from torpor. I have learned well the lessons of corruption. For I have turned my hand to the corruption of the Voivode's resurrection. In the guise of offering wise council to Vukodlak, I poison his heart against those who could speed his resurrection. I accuse the faithful of disloyalty. I accuse the powerful of dangerous ambition. I praise the weak and ineffectual, like the merchant toad Orsi, as obedient servants. Vukodlak's fear of losing power is far greater than his hope of gaining new power, and so he believes every doubt I whisper in his ear. And so he crushed his own resurrection.
Samuel_40_4_1576                Samuel_40_4_1576                Wow. She sacrificed her innocence to stop Vukodlak!
Vukodlak_40_5_1592              Vukodlak_40_5_1592              You survived my hosts. Impressive, young Crusader. You have earned something few have seen. Your final vision shall be that of my true form. Watch as I reveal the absolute power of the Voivode!
Christof_5_1_123                Christof_5_1_123                Blasphemy! Let the builders of this mockery of a holy cathedral show their wicked faces. Out, blaspheming wretch! Taste the steel of righteousness!
Ahzra_5_1_124                   Ahzra_5_1_124                   I am Ahzra, the Unliving, and I bid thee welcome to my demesne. Although I have found thee an inhospitable and ungrateful guest.
Christof_5_1_125                Christof_5_1_125                God strike thee down she-devil! Accursed creature! Earth has no room for such as thee!
Ahzra_5_1_126                   Ahzra_5_1_126                   Nay, verily, all the Earth is our dominion! With every passing day our numbers swell. We shatter all defenses and corrupt all holy places. The Tzimisce shall rule all of Prague, and everyone-- mortal and Cainite alike-- will tremble at our coming!
Christof_5_1_127                Christof_5_1_127                Hell-spawn! The purity of the holy places protects them from thy evil!
Ahzra_5_1_128                   Ahzra_5_1_128                   Purity can be sullied, and holiness can be defiled. The Methuselah of Vysehrad shall awaken soon. He shall defile all that is pure, and leave corruption in his wake. In defilement lies his strength. Purity is sweet meat to him, as thy rich blood is sweet wine to me.
Christof_5_1_129                Christof_5_1_129                Die, thou damned beast! God hath spat thee out of Heaven in ancient times, and now I scourge thee from the Earth. Die, and burn far from the sight of God!
Christof_5_1_131                Christof_5_1_131                Farewell, foul misery. I offer thee no last rites. The sweet redemption of our Lord is forbidden to the likes of thee. Thou hast no home now but the eternal torments of Hell!
StJohn3_5_1_2507                StJohn3_5_1_2507                Who comes there? Behold! The great Crusader ends his day in retreat!  Back for another rest in the nunnery?
StJohn4_5_1_2508                StJohn4_5_1_2508                Between wisdom and courage, 'tis better to be wise.
Christof_5_1_132                Christof_5_1_132                An amulet of Saint Jude. 'Tis an omen. If I redeem so holy a relic from this ungodly tomb, surely the Saint shall grant me his favor.
Christof_5_1_114                Christof_5_1_114                The odor of freshly mined earth is laden with the foul stench of death.
Christof_5_1_115                Christof_5_1_115                What mystery lies moldering here? This poor soul died without a wound, save a bit of blood at the throat.
Christof_5_1_116                Christof_5_1_116                What mockery of a man comes here? Back to the abyss, miscreant!
Christof_5_1_113                Christof_5_1_113                These carts still hold their ore. The diggers have fled this place in haste. And the fiends have not seen fit to continue such honest labor.
Christof_5_1_120                Christof_5_1_120                Wild dogs? Nay! Rats!
Christof_5_1_122                Christof_5_1_122                I reclaim this treasure from the clutches of evil, and reconsecrate it to a godly purpose.
Anezka_6_3_139                  Anezka_6_3_139                  Good morrow, milord. We give thanks that the mines are restored to godly condition, and we rejoice to see thee in health after such danger.
Christof_6_3_140                Christof_6_3_140                Yet there is a danger in health, for I must forgo thy tender ministrations.
Anezka_6_3_141                  Anezka_6_3_141                  Milord!
Christof_6_3_142                Christof_6_3_142                Yet now I suffer a new affliction. For which thou hast the only cure.
Anezka_6_3_143                  Anezka_6_3_143                  I shall serve thee in any way I can, milord.
Christof_6_3_145                Christof_6_3_145                I desire...I have retrieved a sacred relic, milady. The amulet of Saint Jude. I wouldst give it thee, milady, for thy devoted care.
Anezka_6_3_146                  Anezka_6_3_146                  I take no gifts. The Lord's work is favor enough.
Christof_6_3_147                Christof_6_3_147                It is the dearest of all I have, and thou art the dearest of all I have not. Please keep this for my sake.
DABart1_6_1_2513                DABart1_6_1_2513                Behold! 'Tis the Crusader! The demon-slayer who purified the mines! Raise your cups in his honor! To Christof!
StJohn3_6_1_2509                StJohn3_6_1_2509                Who comes there? Ah, the Crusader ends his day in retreat. Back for another rest in the nunnery?
Christof_6_1_2510               Christof_6_1_2510               All of Prague may rest. I have claimed the head of the demon queen.
StJohn4_6_1_2511                StJohn4_6_1_2511                Praise be to Christof! Champion of Prague!
Christof_6_2_133                Christof_6_2_133                I am newly returned from the silver mines. The evil therein is no more.
Geza_6_2_134                    Geza_6_2_134                    The splendor of the Lord rains down upon thee, Christof. I give thee this wrought-silver cross for thy labors.
Christof_6_2_135                Christof_6_2_135                I would hear all thou knowest of vampire lore, that I may fight them more wisely.
Geza_6_2_136                    Geza_6_2_136                    They are evil. That is all thou need knowest. I do not corrupt my soul with knowledge beyond that
Christof_6_2_137                Christof_6_2_137                I thank thee.
Christof_6_3_138                Christof_6_3_138                Good morrow, milady.
Geza_6_4_148                    Geza_6_4_148                    Ahem!
Christof_6_4_149                Christof_6_4_149                Good day, Archbishop Geza.
Geza_6_4_150                    Geza_6_4_150                    Explain thy intentions toward good Sister Anezka! I command thee to speak only truth before God!
Christof_6_4_151                Christof_6_4_151                I find her the fairest of all God's creatures, and bemoan the cruel fate that hath placed her beyond my reach. In another time and place we might have found happiness together, as man and wife.
Geza_6_4_152                    Geza_6_4_152                    Such thoughts are evil! Thy concupiscence damns thy soul and dooms Anezka! I command thee purge such lusts from thy heart, if not for thy sake, then for hers! Do not tempt her away from her holy orders, thou wicked, selfish boy! She is out of thy star, and is not for the likes of thee. Her place is with me! With us. Here in the convent. And thy place is out in the wilderness, striking the fear of God into the hearts of the heathen. If thou art strong enough to entertain such lusts, thou art strong enough to return to thy regiment.
Christof_6_4_153                Christof_6_4_153                Alas, they are too far away.
Geza_6_4_154                    Geza_6_4_154                    Humph. Well, the streets of Prague are still host to unclean demons. Perhaps thou shouldst destroy these abominations  to expend some of thy hot blood!
Christof_6_4_155                Christof_6_4_155                I must protest. My place is here. The demon queen confessed they sought to defile holy places. 'Tis my duty to protect these hallowed grounds when next they attack.
Geza_6_4_156                    Geza_6_4_156                    Thy Archbishop shall dictate thy duty! Hunt them in the streets.
Christof_6_4_157                Christof_6_4_157                I obey.
Christof_6_4_158                Christof_6_4_158                I obey. But I shall protect the streets in front of the Cathedral and Convent as well!
Geza_6_4_159                    Geza_6_4_159                    Harumph!
Geza_6_4_160                    Geza_6_4_160                    I shall be watching thee, thou immoral lad!
Prague4_6_5_161                 Prague4_6_5_161                 Hurrah!
Prague4_6_5_162                 Prague4_6_5_162                 Hurrah for Christof, the Swordbrethren!
Prague4_6_5_163                 Prague4_6_5_163                 The mines are safe again! All hail the crusading hero of Prague!
Stranger_6_5_164                Stranger_6_5_164                Hurrah!
Prague3_6_5_165                 Prague3_6_5_165                 We thank thee for restoring the mines. We finally have new silver to craft and sell!
Prague3_6_5_166                 Prague3_6_5_166                 God bless thee, Sir Christof.
Christof_6_5_167                Christof_6_5_167                My heart warms to see the good people rejoice so, and yet Ahzra's words chill me to the core. I fear that evil endures in Prague.
Jiri_6_7_183                    Jiri_6_7_183                    The bells of Prague do ring out yer victory, Christof! The whole city prizes yer handiwork!
Christof_6_7_184                Christof_6_7_184                And I prize thine.
Christof_6_6_171                Christof_6_6_171                Know thou of the Tzimisce?
Christof_6_6_172                Christof_6_6_172                Know thou of the Methuselah of Vysehrad?
Christof_6_6_173                Christof_6_6_173                What means she by Cainite?
Christof_6_6_174                Christof_6_6_174                What lore hast thou of these demons?
Unorna_6_6_175                  Unorna_6_6_175                  Of the thirteen great vampire clans, the fiends of Clan Tzimisce be the most vicious. They come from the dark Carpathian Mountains where ancient evil slumbers. They seek to rule mortals as cattle, and abduct us from the streets at night. They cruelly craft the flesh to fashion monsters, which they call szlachta. To extend their rule into daylight, they do corrupt mortals with wealth and power and their own demonic blood. When the mortal servants do drink the blood of their masters, they become ghouls that live forever- so long as they drink each day their daily blood. In the oldest ghoul lineages, like the royal Premysl family, children are born as Revenants- mortals born with vampiric blood coursing in their veins.
Unorna_6_6_176                  Unorna_6_6_176                  Methuselahs are among the most ancient and powerful of all vampires. Alas, I know nothing of the Methuselah of Vysehrad, save that Vysehrad Castle is the foul heart of the evil Tzimisce vampire Clan. But don't ye be going there, young one. Even an army of thy Swordbrethren could not dislodge such evil.
Unorna_6_6_177                  Unorna_6_6_177                  Though every mother's child knows of vampires, very few know they are descended from Caine, and raised by the first woman created by God.
Christof_6_6_178                Christof_6_6_178                Do not blaspheme so about Adam and Eve!
Unorna_6_6_179                  Unorna_6_6_179                  Not Adam and Eve! Adam... and Lilith! Lilith was Adam's first wife, and a spirited woman was she! Now Adam wanted Lilith to serve under him, and Lilith spurned such bumpkin courtship, leaving him for the company of angels and devils. So God did fashion Eve, who was meek and mild. But their children were far from gentle. When Caine slew his brother Abel, God cursed Caine, saying that the food that grew of the earth would no longer sustain him. Only blood, like the blood he spilled from Abel, would slake his thirst. Lilith found the outcast Caine, and taught him to harness the powers of Heaven and Hell. God made Cain the first Vampire, and Cain made all the rest, by preying on the descendants of Seth, Adam's third son. And so all vampires are called Cainites, but only by those who know more than the church tells.
Christof_6_6_180                Christof_6_6_180                I cannot pay heed to such strange notions.
Unorna_6_6_181                  Unorna_6_6_181                  You will heed me if ye would halt them!
Unorna_6_6_182                  Unorna_6_6_182                  The vampires do prey on mortals, but they do save their real hatred for other vampires. The thirteen great vampire clans are forever at war- clan against clan, Elder against Childer in a fearsome Jyhad. They do shake the world to its foundations with their hate, and twist up the destiny of mortals into their dark tapestry.
Christof_6_6_2556               Christof_6_6_2556               I thank thee.
Unorna_6_6_168                  Unorna_6_6_168                  I bid ye welcome, and sing yer praises for the slaying of Ahzra the Unliving.
Christof_6_6_169                Christof_6_6_169                Without thy lore I could not have slain her, so I thank thee for thy wise counsel. But Ahzra said many things that did shake my soul. I would have benefit of thy lore.
Unorna_6_6_170                  Unorna_6_6_170                  Ask thy questions.
Rev4_6_8_185                    Rev4_6_8_185                    Oooh, 'tis the little warrior!
Rev3_6_8_186                    Rev3_6_8_186                    I bet yer soft and tender inside yer tin britches!
Rev1_6_8_187                    Rev1_6_8_187                    I'll eat yer gizzard while it's still attached to yer quivering body!
Rev1_6_8_188                    Rev1_6_8_188                    This is thy payment for bringing final death to Ahzra the Unliving!
Nun_7_1_190                     Nun_7_1_190                     I-yeeeeeeee!
Geza_7_1_191                    Geza_7_1_191                    Christof! Thou hast brought evil into this holy place! This taint follows thy soiled soul!
Rev2_7_1_192                    Rev2_7_1_192                    Arr! It burns!
Rev1_7_1_193                    Rev1_7_1_193                    The she-cow's vile purity burns like unto the sun!
Christof_7_1_194                Christof_7_1_194                Back to the abyss, hellspawn! Thou art anathema in the eyes of God! Die! And be damned!
Anezka_7_1_195                  Anezka_7_1_195                  Away with thee! We forbid thee to defile this holy place!
Christof_7_3_213                Christof_7_3_213                Art thou planning mischief inside this holy place, thou wasted mockery of a man?
Rev1_7_3_214                    Rev1_7_3_214                    'Tis the hated one! Kill him, and Master Vukodlak will elevate us all!
Christof_7_3_215                Christof_7_3_215                Thou shall not elevate, but shall descend. To the infernal pit! Where the angels shall laugh at thy torment!
Anezka_7_2_196                  Anezka_7_2_196                  It appears I am once again in thy debt, brave sir, for coming to us in our time of need
Christof_7_2_197                Christof_7_2_197                As I am in thy debt, for saving my life with thy tender care. And for thy uncommon valor in standing with me 'gainst the forces of hell.
Anezka_7_2_198                  Anezka_7_2_198                  I pray, milord, that thou art always near, to deliver us from this evil.
Christof_7_2_199                Christof_7_2_199                Milady, I pledge my arm to thy cause, for as long as I draw breath, and beyond.
Anezka_7_2_200                  Anezka_7_2_200                  Then I have faith in the safety of our humble convent.
Ecat_7_2_201                    Ecat_7_2_201                    His skill is great, and he fights with the strength of a demon.
Cosmas_7_2_202                  Cosmas_7_2_202                  His courage has opened the mines again. Think of how the gift of Caine would enhance the might of such a man!
Ecat_7_2_203                    Ecat_7_2_203                    Aye, he has done great service to the Promethian cause by dispatching Ahzra without our involvement. But this decision must be made with surpassing care.
Cosmas_7_2_204                  Cosmas_7_2_204                  With so many of us missing, we need his sword in the coming tumult. The other clans are surely considering him for the Embrace by now.
Ecat_7_2_205                    Ecat_7_2_205                    No doubt. But hast thou forgotten the power the church wields over the minds of these mortals, Cosmas? His faith is the strength behind his steel. Stripped of his faith, would he be of any use to us, I wonder? 
Cosmas_7_2_206                  Cosmas_7_2_206                  He has been cut from the herd by his wound. He's been separated from the heart of his Swordbrethren by many miles. Like any stray, he is more malleable. He may bend rather than break.
Ecat_7_2_207                    Ecat_7_2_207                    Thou art wise, Cosmas. How well thou knowest the hearts of men of war. Yet I am not convinced.
Cosmas_7_2_208                  Cosmas_7_2_208                  The Premysls are enraged at his continuing interference in their plans. I have learned that they have now placed a rich bounty upon his head. He'll not outlive the next moon.
Ecat_7_2_209                    Ecat_7_2_209                    Get thee to the Prince, and tell him that this night Ecaterina the Wise awakens another into darkness.
Cosmas_7_2_210                  Cosmas_7_2_210                  Thy will is done.
Ecat_7_2_211                    Ecat_7_2_211                    And Cosmas?
Ecat_7_2_212                    Ecat_7_2_212                    Should this Christof falter or fail, he must fall.
Geza_6_9_189                    Geza_6_9_189                    Thy business is not within this holy place, but without it. Get thee hence and protect the streets from the devil's children.
Christof_7_1_2514               Christof_7_1_2514               Alarums from within the convent! Anezka! I will protect thee, the Archbishop be damned!
Christof_7_6_268                Christof_7_6_268                Water... Anezka? Water I beg of thee... I thirst... I starve...
Ecat_7_6_269                    Ecat_7_6_269                    I am Ecaterina the Wise, Ecaterina the Promethian, Ecaterina the leader of the Brujah clan. We are the children of Caine, and now thou art one of us.
Christof_7_6_270                Christof_7_6_270                God have mercy on my soul! I am damned!
Cosmas_7_6_271                  Cosmas_7_6_271                  Steady now, whelp! Thou lackst the strength to fight, and I would slice thee in twain without hesitation!
Ecat_7_6_272                    Ecat_7_6_272                    Thou must feed, my Childe. . Before the hunger overtakes thee and drives thee mad.
Christof_7_6_273                Christof_7_6_273                Am I now a miserable demon, stealing blood to live, damned and cursed to Hell? Is this all that remains of noble Christof?
Ecat_7_6_274                    Ecat_7_6_274                    Nay, my Childe. There is far more to thee than ever before. Thou art Brujah now. We Brujah are the philosophers of Caine's lineage. Where other Clans treasured gold and trinkets, we sought pure wisdom. We strove to unravel the thorny knots that bind the very gates of Heaven. Are we damned? Nay, we are exalted! In fabled Carthage we lived in concordance with all mortals. Together we sought the secrets of Eden, that the children of Caine and the children of Seth would be raised up. But the hated Ventrue grew jealous of our power. The Patrician Clan betrayed us, and tried to destroy us. We survived, but fabled Carthage did not. Despite this great loss, we Promethians continue our search for harmony with all mortals.
Cosmas_7_6_275                  Cosmas_7_6_275                  We are the most noble of our breed, Christof. We seek only the fulfillment of the dream that was Carthage!  But we need thy help to achieve this goal! A war lies ahead--- a terrible battle that calls for warriors like us!
Christof_7_6_276                Christof_7_6_276                Wars among devils do not concern me!
Cosmas_7_6_277                  Cosmas_7_6_277                  We will cause thee to think otherwise, whelp!
Ecat_7_6_278                    Ecat_7_6_278                    Enough Cosmas! The Brujah blood needs time to cool. Come; let us leave our young one to think upon his situation. But know this, Christof, thou would have been killed or embraced by our enemies on this night. We have given thee life eternal, and awakened thee to the true face of thy world. I do not expect gratitude, but our clan requires loyalty or death.
Christof_7_6_279                Christof_7_6_279                There is no life in me. Even my heart has ceased to beat. My very soul has been stripped from me. There is nothing left for salvation or damnation!
Christof_7_4_216                Christof_7_4_216                Milady Anezka. I must speak with thee.
Anezka_7_4_217                  Anezka_7_4_217                  Hush! Lest thou rouse the convent with thy clamor.
Christof_7_4_218                Christof_7_4_218                I have wandered the city for hours, yet each turn of the streets did lead me back here.
Anezka_7_4_219                  Anezka_7_4_219                  My lord, 'tis dusk! Thou shouldst not be out of doors! Fiends shall soon be legion!
Christof_7_4_220                Christof_7_4_220                I would fight all fiends of hell to stand at thy door.
Anezka_7_4_221                  Anezka_7_4_221                  And..and how may our holy order assist his lordship this eve?
Christof_7_4_222                Christof_7_4_222                Milady, thou must hear me. I sought to tell thee once before, but fear took my voice.
Anezka_7_4_223                  Anezka_7_4_223                  What power could daunt the man who hath faced demons from hell?
Christof_7_4_224                Christof_7_4_224                The face of an angel from heaven.
Anezka_7_4_225                  Anezka_7_4_225                  Oh. Milord, I...
Christof_7_4_226                Christof_7_4_226                Thou hast stuck me a wound that heals not. My life's blood pours out before me and I cannot stanch it. I do love thee, fair Anezka! I do love thee with a passion I have never known!
Anezka_7_4_227                  Anezka_7_4_227                  Milord, I must protest! Such love is an affront to Heaven.
Christof_7_4_228                Christof_7_4_228                If thou deny me I am undone!
Anezka_7_4_229                  Anezka_7_4_229                  I... I cannot deny thee. I too am afflicted with a wound for which thou art the only cure.
Christof_7_4_230                Christof_7_4_230                Ah...
Anezka_7_4_237                  Anezka_7_4_237                  My soul fell when first I beheld thee. And now my heart is cleft in twain. My love of God and my love of thee have torn me to ribbons, for I must betray one to keep faith with the other.
Christof_7_4_238                Christof_7_4_238                Thus my sins do pour out before me, for I have spilt my wickedness upon thy innocent bosom.
Anezka_7_4_239                  Anezka_7_4_239                  Nay! 'Tis my wanton desire that hath ensnared thy virtuous heart. I was a fool to confess my heart to thee. Gladly would I imperil mine own soul, but never thine!
Christof_7_4_240                Christof_7_4_240                Then truly we are lost. Our souls are beyond all hope of redemption.
Anezka_7_4_241                  Anezka_7_4_241                  Milord! Look thou, at the locket thou didst give me. 'Tis the locket of Saint Jude. The patron saint of lost causes.
Christof_7_4_242                Christof_7_4_242                Thus fate makes a bitter mockery of our plight!
Anezka_7_4_243                  Anezka_7_4_243                  Say not so! Where the saints tread, hope follows. Mayhap Saint Jude shall bring us hope past all hope. I pray we find a way.
Christof_7_4_244                Christof_7_4_244                I pray so too. Thy hopeful words have filled my heart with courage, and I can leave satisfied. Farewell, fair Anezka.
Anezka_7_4_245                  Anezka_7_4_245                  Nay! The streets are not safe at night! Thou must stay here in the convent 'til the sun shines again.
Christof_7_4_246                Christof_7_4_246                May God have mercy upon my soul, for the Devil has just placed into my heart the most impure of notions. I do tremble to confess it, but I feel I would cast away all holiness to stay. I would exchange an eternity of Heaven's bliss for one night by thy side.
Anezka_7_4_247                  Anezka_7_4_247                  Know thee that thou art not alone in such desires.
Christof_7_4_248                Christof_7_4_248                Then truly, I am damned! I am condemned for my lust, but I must not drag thee into the abyss with me. I must flee this holy place, and return no more.
Anezka_7_4_249                  Anezka_7_4_249                  Milord! Come back!
Cosmas_7_7_1604                 Cosmas_7_7_1604                 Learn thy lessons well.
Ecat_7_7_280                    Ecat_7_7_280                    Thou needs must pledge fealty to thy new Mistress.
Christof_7_7_281                Christof_7_7_281                I do NOT agree to serve thee. I reject thee and shun thy evil works! I take my leave of thee!
Christof_7_7_282                Christof_7_7_282                I am newly remade, and know little of this world. I agree only to follow thy lead for the present.
Christof_7_7_283                Christof_7_7_283                I submit to thee in all things. I am thine to command.
Ecat_7_7_284                    Ecat_7_7_284                    Such rebelliousness is unseemly in a Brujah. I do not like such willfulness in my Childer.
Ecat_7_7_287                    Ecat_7_7_287                    Thou knowest well thy place.
Ecat_7_7_290                    Ecat_7_7_290                    Insolent whelp! I forbid it! Tempt not my wrath.
Cosmas_7_7_291                  Cosmas_7_7_291                  The man he was can do little else, Ecaterina.
Cosmas_7_7_285                  Cosmas_7_7_285                  He displays as much fealty as can be expected from such a one, Ecaterina.
Cosmas_7_7_288                  Cosmas_7_7_288                  Humph. I had expected more fire in him. Mayhap thou misjudged his spine, Ecaterina.
Ecat_7_7_292                    Ecat_7_7_292                    The vampire he is must do otherwise, Cosmas.
Ecat_7_7_289                    Ecat_7_7_289                    We shall see. Mayhap he takes instruction with more spirit.
Ecat_7_7_286                    Ecat_7_7_286                    Very well, Cosmas. Then thou may hold his leash. We shall see if he takes instruction with more deference.
Cosmas_7_7_293                  Cosmas_7_7_293                  He will learn...should he survive his own folly.
Ecat_7_7_294                    Ecat_7_7_294                    Thou speakest sagely, Cosmas. Very well, Christof. I shall not strike thee down for this one act of disobedience. And if thou leavest now, I shall not move to stop thee. But neither shall I lift my hand to save thee from reaping the fruits of such disobedience.
Christof_7_7_2515               Christof_7_7_2515               Then, farewell, fair cruelty. I take my leave of thy damnable house and my own damnable un-life! For only in death can I find solace for the loss of my soul!
Wilhem_7_7_300                  Wilhem_7_7_300                  Such a death would not end thy accursed existence but curse it even further. Shouldst thou ever meet a wraith, thou would understand that thy present torment is paradise compared to the anguish of those in the Shadowlands. Thou dost owe me a great debt for saving thee from a far greater damnation. And for that debt of life, I desire that thou heed the word of thy Mistress Ecaterina- at least until thou hast sense enough to come in out of the sun. Do that, and guard my back on an important mission, and I will consider our accounts settled. Now let us return to Ecaterina.
Ecat_7_7_302                    Ecat_7_7_302                    Christof, thou must understand that our enemies are legion. And none more vile than Clan Tzimisce, which infests Prague like a plague. The fiends seek thy death in payment for the slaying of Ahzra and her szlachta servitors. If thou are to survive, thou must quickly master the powers of thy blood. Thou must pay heed to all our laws, or thou wilt become a gibbering monster like the Tzimisce. Our first law-
Wilhem_7_7_303                  Wilhem_7_7_303                  Mistress Ecaterina, I crave thy indulgence. Christof is a man of action. His learning shall be the greater on the field of battle than the parlors of the Promethians. Let me take him on my mission to Garinol's Haven, where he may learn our ways first hand. I could use a stout broadsword protecting my back.
Ecat_7_7_304                    Ecat_7_7_304                    Christof is not yet ready.
Wilhem_7_7_305                  Wilhem_7_7_305                  Verily, he is as ready as he shall ever be.
Ecat_7_7_306                    Ecat_7_7_306                    I would have thy counsel, Cosmas.
Cosmas_7_7_307                  Cosmas_7_7_307                  Christof is no Brujah philosopher. His hot blood cools too much when he has no mission, and curdles. Send him forth. If he survives, he will have mastered our ways.
Ecat_7_7_308                    Ecat_7_7_308                    Very well. Christof, go thou with Wilhem Streicher. Trust what he says if thou wouldst outlive the night. Go, my swordsmen, and strike deep into the lair of our dear ally, Garinol the Cappadocian.
Wilhem_7_7_309                  Wilhem_7_7_309                  Time to cut thy teeth, fledgling.
Wilhem_7_8_310                  Wilhem_7_8_310                  Ecaterina is a scholar, not a warrior born. She would keep her fledglings studying Vampire lore in her nest till Gehenna comes, and never hurl them from the nest to test their wings. But I cannot spend hours debating Cainite wisdom in her University. Give me a stout broadsword in my hand and the sweet taste of my foe's blood on my tongue, and I have all the wisdom I need. And I suspect thou art akin to me.
Christof_7_8_311                Christof_7_8_311                I am not akin to thee, blood-guzzler. I strike only those that deserve my steel. I do not murder the innocents to gorge on their blood!
Wilhem_7_8_312                  Wilhem_7_8_312                  Thou shalt feed upon blood or thou shalt die. But thou need not kill to do so. Drink only so much blood as will sustain thee. Suffer thy prey to live on, for to kill during the feeding is to violate our Promethian ethic. And that way lies the Beast.
Christof_7_8_313                Christof_7_8_313                The Beast?
Wilhem_7_8_314                  Wilhem_7_8_314                  A Beast born of Caine's sin doth coil within thy breast, Christof. Keep it at bay or be lost forever. Acts of cruelty unleash that Beast, until it rules thee, as it rules many of Ahzra's kin.
Christof_7_8_315                Christof_7_8_315                The Beast made them madmen?
Wilhem_7_8_316                  Wilhem_7_8_316                  Nay, the mad vampires are called Malkavian. Shouldst thou meet one, God help thee. Shouldst thou need his assistance, God cannot help thee. Nay, the Beast is not mere madness, but demonic possession by the curse of Caine. We Promethians are wayfarers in the land of the Beast, but we stray not from the Road of Humanity- the Via Humanitas. By our acts of compassion are we saved from the jaws of the Beast. Remember this always, or be devoured from within.
Wilhem_7_8_2516                 Wilhem_7_8_2516                 Thou hast life everlasting, so long as thou keepest thy pallid flesh hidden from the sun's hateful rays. The life-giving sun only brings death to the likes of us. Each day we must retreat to our haven in the chambers below the University.
Wilhem_7_8_324                  Wilhem_7_8_324                  The curse of Caine also brings strange blessings. Behold!
Wilhem_7_8_325                  Wilhem_7_8_325                  Try it!
Christof_7_8_326                Christof_7_8_326                Yahhhh!
Wilhem_7_8_327                  Wilhem_7_8_327                  Ha! Thou hast much to learn to master the Blood of Caine.
Christof_7_8_318                Christof_7_8_318                I grow faint!
Wilhem_7_8_319                  Wilhem_7_8_319                  The hunger overcomes thee. The Beast strengthens as thou weaken from lack of blood. Canst thou feel him uncoiling in thy heart? Thou must feed.
Wilhem_7_8_320                  Wilhem_7_8_320                  Feed or die, Christof! For I shall kill thee before I let the Beast claim thee!
Wilhem_7_8_321                  Wilhem_7_8_321                  Only what thou needs, Christof! No more!
Wilhem_7_8_322                  Wilhem_7_8_322                  Behold. His wound vanishes.
Wilhem_7_8_323                  Wilhem_7_8_323                  Monsieur shouldn't drink so much wine, eh Christof? Do not worry, he'll wake up and know nothing of what has happened. Most mortals find great pleasure in the dark kiss. Ecaterina tells us that in Carthage the mortals happily submitted to it.
Christof_7_8_328                Christof_7_8_328                These powers tempt me to dark acts. How easily could I rain devastation on all who vex me.
Wilhem_7_8_329                  Wilhem_7_8_329                  Thy fear and thy power conspire to make of thee a Beast. Seek thee an anchor for thy humanity- a rock to cling to when storms come.
Christof_7_8_330                Christof_7_8_330                Anezka...
Wilhem_7_8_331                  Wilhem_7_8_331                  Love for a mortal is the most dangerous of all anchors.
Christof_7_8_332                Christof_7_8_332                I must see Anezka.
Wilhem_7_8_333                  Wilhem_7_8_333                  That is most unwise. The cheerful world of daylight is lost to thee.
Christof_7_8_334                Christof_7_8_334                I must see her again! Just to look upon her for a moment!
Wilhem_7_8_335                  Wilhem_7_8_335                  Ecaterina would not be pleased...  Oh, very well. Shouldst thou display valor in our mission this night, I shall take thee to the convent. But only for a moment to glimpse her as she sleeps. Now come. Our mission lies ahead.
Christof_8_1_2517               Christof_8_1_2517               Forgive me, Wilhem. I was a fool to place my trust in yon villain Mercurio.
Wilhem_8_1_2518                 Wilhem_8_1_2518                 Many Cainites will seek to lure thee with promise of the life thou lost. Beware such promises. There is no redemption for us.
Ecat_8_5_424                    Ecat_8_5_424                    Christof, thou must do my bidding. Should'st thou flee our Haven, our enemies would rend thee asunder for the pleasure of seeing thy Brujah blood run in rivulets down the paving stones.
Christof_8_5_425                Christof_8_5_425                And why should I not die? I crave the solace of the sepulcher; the peace that only clamorous death may bring.
Ecat_8_5_426                    Ecat_8_5_426                    Has not thy Lord forbidden it?
Christof_8_5_427                Christof_8_5_427                Alas, thou speaks true. The Almighty commands that I not perish by mine own hand. And yet, have I not perished already? Can the dead die again?
Ecat_8_5_428                    Ecat_8_5_428                    How simple is thy creed. Does it stand when tested by the complexity of the world? Thy laws apply only to thy life and thy death. Not thy unlife.
Christof_8_5_429                Christof_8_5_429                I live on, yet I am not alive. I have died, yet I am not dead. Though I have perished, my soul is neither in heaven nor hell, though hell this seems.
Ecat_8_5_430                    Ecat_8_5_430                    And so thou must continue to follow the law 'gainst self-slaughter, even in un-death.
Christof_8_5_431                Christof_8_5_431                I can not—I dare not-- give up this un-life until I am certain of the fate of my soul. The Devil may have my soul now, but if there is a way to recover my soul, I am compelled to find it. Though my state is damned, I am cursed to continue it, lest I face a greater damnation. And so I must shun the tranquility of the tomb, and cling to this unquiet grave.
Ecat_8_1_1603                   Ecat_8_1_1603                   Do not disobey me, childe!
Ecat_8_1_1970                   Ecat_8_1_1970                   Back to moon over thy cruel fate? Seek consolation and purpose in thy task, my Childe.
Ecat_8_1_1971                   Ecat_8_1_1971                   Thou hast not yet completed thy tasks. Go ye hence, Christof. We shall speak more when thou art ready.
Wilhem_8_1_337                  Wilhem_8_1_337                  Behold Petrin Hill Monastery. Haven of the Cappadocian vampires in Prague.
Christof_8_1_338                Christof_8_1_338                The monks are vampires?
Wilhem_8_1_339                  Wilhem_8_1_339                  Nay. The Cappadocians lurk in the desecrated crypts below the monastery, out of sight of the monks who provide them with fresh blood. Only those monks that serve Garinol know the real master of the monastery.
Christof_8_2_340                Christof_8_2_340                What is our mission?
Wilhem_8_2_341                  Wilhem_8_2_341                  A mission of misfortune. Wise and noble Garinol is friend and ally to Ecaterina. I take no pleasure in storming his home. But Ecaterina has divined that the Cappadocians have stolen from her. We seek to retrieve a precious fragment of the Book of Nod- the wisdom of the ancient Cainites.
Christof_8_2_342                Christof_8_2_342                Why would Garinol steal from her?
Wilhem_8_2_343                  Wilhem_8_2_343                  We know not. Until we learn all, we cannot let them know we serve Ecaterina.
Unorna_0_0_1926                 Unorna_0_0_1926                 Hello young one. What is it you... but hold. You do look so strangely... They have hurt you! The Cainites have taken ye for their own! Oh, my poor boy. But fear not. Unorna neither fears ye, nor casts ye out. I'll help ye if I can.
Christof_8_2_347                Christof_8_2_347                More dark worship. Is there no end to the vampiric taste for blasphemy?
Wilhem_8_2_348                  Wilhem_8_2_348                  Garinol celebrates the transfiguration of death. But I do not share his fondness for the grave. I have died once to become a Cainite, and do not seek to die again.
Wilhem_8_2_349                  Wilhem_8_2_349                  Behold! The mural depicts Caine as God's favored son. Every child knows that Caine's sacrifice of grain vexed the Lord, and Abel's sacrifice of the blood of a lamb satisfied the Lord. But mysterious old Cainites tell us that the Lord craved blood, and reveled in Caine's second sacrifice: the blood of his brother Abel. The Lord raised up Caine over the other sons of Adam. He freed him from plowing the earth for food, and set a mark on him that he live forever. Garinol devoutly believes this Cainite heresy, which holds that the curse of Caine is truly a blessing.
Christof_8_2_350                Christof_8_2_350                And thou? Dost thou believe the Cainite heresy?
Wilhem_8_2_351                  Wilhem_8_2_351                  If I am favored of the Lord, the angels have not seen fit to tell me of it.
Cappa1_8_2_362                  Cappa1_8_2_362                  We have cut a shroud for thee. Come and wear it.
Cappa1_8_2_363                  Cappa1_8_2_363                  Thy coffin awaits thee, stripling. The grave yawns to swallow thee!
Cappa1_8_2_364                  Cappa1_8_2_364                  Thy mind is troubled, warrior. Let us soothe the tumult in thy mind with the comfort of the tomb.
Cappa1_8_2_361                  Cappa1_8_2_361                  Feel the cold embrace of the tomb, whelp!
Christof_8_2_365                Christof_8_2_365                These claws are like unto the gnarled fingers of the she-fiend Ahzra!
Christof_8_2_353                Christof_8_2_353                Thou dost fight thy allies with great zeal, Wilhem.
Wilhem_8_2_354                  Wilhem_8_2_354                  I take no pleasure in it. I just do what must be done. The only one I will not fight is Garinol, the leader of the Cappadocians. I chose to strike this night because Garinol and his assistant Serena have an audience with the Cainite Prince of Prague, Rudolf Brandl, and will not be here.
Wilhem_8_2_352                  Wilhem_8_2_352                  Final Death to thee, my Cainite brothers! The oblivion thou seeks is upon thee!
Christof_8_2_357                Christof_8_2_357                An earthen floor blankets this room.
Wilhem_8_2_358                  Wilhem_8_2_358                  Aye, the Cappadocians do bury their newly embraced in a ritual of death.
Christof_8_3_387                Christof_8_3_387                What means this dire portent?
Wilhem_8_3_388                  Wilhem_8_3_388                  This is a great secret, known to a few wise vampires. The Book of Nod tells us that the thirteen ancient founders of the vampire clans will one day rise from their age-old sleep. When these Antediluvians emerge from torpor, they shall fall upon us, their descendants, and gorge their unholy thirst upon our blood. The day they rise is called Gehenna, or the Winnowing, and it signals the end of all that is. We know not how to prevent it, but even now Ecaterina searches for a way.
Christof_8_3_389                Christof_8_3_389                Why didst thou not tell me of this? With such evil barking at our heels, we must hasten our work!
Wilhem_8_3_390                  Wilhem_8_3_390                  Forgive us. Most young Cainites are not ready to hear it. They mistrust their Sire, and that we cannot have.
Wilhem_8_2_370                  Wilhem_8_2_370                  I would have words with this Mercurio. Venerable Garinol should not have to rely upon an untrustworthy Childe.
Christof_8_2_371                Christof_8_2_371                If Mercurio is charged with the protection of this Haven, I wonder that we have not yet faced him.
Christof_8_2_367                Christof_8_2_367                A key. A key in the form of a finger bone.
Wilhem_8_2_368                  Wilhem_8_2_368                  A key. A key in the form of a finger bone.
Wilhem_8_3_385                  Wilhem_8_3_385                  Garinol's search for wisdom has not trespassed into our Haven. 'Twas his false servant, Mercurio. We were fortunate to find the fragment before another Clan could learn of it. Others might not have spared Garinol in the search.
Mercurio_8_3_373                Mercurio_8_3_373                What? How comes thou here? Flee now, and the great Mercurio will not smite thee!
Wilhem_8_3_374                  Wilhem_8_3_374                  So, hiding like a rat in a hole whilst thy kin fight valiantly in defense of thy theft? Thou art a clot-pole of a coward.
Mercurio_8_3_375                Mercurio_8_3_375                I hide not. My work is too important. Work thou hast interrupted. I shall crack open the Tree of Life, and uncover all the secrets of death and life!
Christof_8_3_377                Christof_8_3_377                Canst thou restore mortal life to a vampire?
Christof_8_3_376                Christof_8_3_376                I shall tell thee the secret of death and life. Give us the Nod fragment, and we shall let thee live.
Mercurio_8_3_378                Mercurio_8_3_378                Soon, very soon shall I have such secrets. Smite thy Brujah ally, and thou shalt be the first to be restored when the secrets of death are mine!
Christof_8_3_379                Christof_8_3_379                I shall smite him. Have at thee, Wilhem.
Christof_8_3_380                Christof_8_3_380                Nay. Thou art false. Give us the Nod fragment, or my blade shall teach thee the secrets of death.
Mercurio_8_3_383                Mercurio_8_3_383                Nay! Thou wilt tell Garinol of my theft. 'Tis better I bury thee in a secret grave, with stakes of oak piercing thy hearts, and Wraith-worms shall hollow out thy noble brows and robust cheeks.
Wilhem_8_3_381                  Wilhem_8_3_381                  Traitor! Thy treachery gives way to the Beast!
Mercurio_8_3_382                Mercurio_8_3_382                Here is thy secret of life: trusting souls deserve it not!
Wilhem_8_3_391                  Wilhem_8_3_391                  We must hurry. Dawn approaches. The sun is our destroyer now, Christof. Remember this. During the day thou must find a place to rest where no sunlight can enter. Let us return to the University, where Ecaterina shall surely celebrate our triumph!
Christof_8_3_392                Christof_8_3_392                I cannot share thy pleasure over such a deed.
Wilhem_8_3_393                  Wilhem_8_3_393                  Come now. Before thy Embrace, did thou not slay evil creatures? Behold! Thy mission survives even thy death!
Christof_8_3_394                Christof_8_3_394                That is scant solace for the loss of my soul.
Wilhem_8_3_395                  Wilhem_8_3_395                  Thou dost brood too much upon events thou cannot change, Christof. Seek consolation in our Promethian mission. Bury thy woe in the blood-drenched soil of battle. I have found that the fury of war weaves enchantments that soothe even the most troubled heart, in Kindred and Kine alike.
Christof_8_3_396                Christof_8_3_396                I well know the seduction of warfare.
Wilhem_8_3_397                  Wilhem_8_3_397                  Then thou knowest the joy of cutting a bloody swath through thy foemen! 
Christof_8_3_398                Christof_8_3_398                The only joy I felt was in doing the work of heaven as a soldier of God. I have done nothing to warrant this fallen state. How can God allow men to become demons? How can God snatch away the promise of salvation?
Wilhem_8_3_399                  Wilhem_8_3_399                  God allows the innocent child on the battlefield to cry and does nothing. So why should God prevent the misery of a killer like unto thee?
Christof_8_3_400                Christof_8_3_400                I do not know. I have not considered these things.
Wilhem_8_3_401                  Wilhem_8_3_401                  Come. I promised thee a trip to the convent. But make haste! Dawn comes quickly!
Christof_8_2_372                Christof_8_2_372                What beast once wore this skull upon its neck?
Wilhem_8_2_344                  Wilhem_8_2_344                  The Cappadocians are a strange clan, Christof. They seek to understand death and the secrets of the grave. Prepare thyself. Their halls are guarded by the walking dead.
Christof_8_2_345                Christof_8_2_345                The stench of death taints this holy place.
Wilhem_8_2_346                  Wilhem_8_2_346                  'Tis fitting for the Clan of Death.
Wilhem_8_2_366                  Wilhem_8_2_366                  'Tis Garinol's study! Now to find the key that unlocks the secrets of the dead.
Christof_8_2_359                Christof_8_2_359                My wounds close of their own accord.
Wilhem_8_2_360                  Wilhem_8_2_360                  'Tis the power of the blood.
Anezka_8_4_402                  Anezka_8_4_402                  Christof?
Anezka_8_4_403                  Anezka_8_4_403                  My love?
Christof_8_4_404                Christof_8_4_404                Thou art accursed if Christof be thy love.
Anezka_8_4_405                  Anezka_8_4_405                  Nay, do not go, Milord.
Anezka_8_4_406                  Anezka_8_4_406                  Let me look upon thee.
Anezka_8_4_407                  Anezka_8_4_407                  Good my lord, forgive me, I was filled with fright to behold thee. Thy comely face is now sicklied over with a pale cast, and I scarcely recognized thee. Forgive me. Thou hast been gone so long we feared for thy life.
Christof_8_4_408                Christof_8_4_408                Thou feared aright. For I am dead to the world, and my soul is lost.
Anezka_8_4_409                  Anezka_8_4_409                  Do I behold a ghost? Nay, I touch thy arm, and I know thou art flesh and blood.
Christof_8_4_410                Christof_8_4_410                Not flesh. Only blood.
Anezka_8_4_411                  Anezka_8_4_411                  My lord, thy words do fill my heart with fear, and I tremble.
Anezka_8_4_412                  Anezka_8_4_412                  Good my lord, why dost thou look so strangely upon me?
Christof_8_4_2495               Christof_8_4_2495               My mind is filled with such thoughts of such base villainy. My hunger for thee is stronger now than when my heart did pump its own warm blood!
Anezka_8_4_2496                 Anezka_8_4_2496                 I know not what misfortune weighs so heavily upon thy soul, or has driven the rosy warmth from thy cheek, but the heavenly powers can banish all such darkness!
Christof_8_4_413                Christof_8_4_413                Should they banish this darkness, they would banish all that is left of me.
Anezka_8_4_414                  Anezka_8_4_414                  Say not so! My lord, I know thy heart, and thy soul is pure!
Christof_8_4_415                Christof_8_4_415                My heart? My soul? Thy words torment me with remembrance of all that I have lost. I was a fool to have come here. I must never return to a house of God.
Anezka_8_4_416                  Anezka_8_4_416                  Nay! I pray thee stay! My honored lord, thy soul is in peril!
Christof_8_4_417                Christof_8_4_417                My honored lady, my soul is lost! My cause is doomed and I am damned!
Christof_8_4_418                Christof_8_4_418                I go! I will not look upon thee again.
Anezka_8_4_419                  Anezka_8_4_419                  Then I pray thee, my lord, receive this token of me. 'Tis but a small amulet of Saint Jude. Thou didst give it me, and it has soothed the pangs of mine own heart that began when first I beheld thee, and a desperate, hopeless love did grow in my breast. Mayhap it will be some consolation to thy doomed cause.
Christof_8_4_420                Christof_8_4_420                Art thou deaf woman? My cause is lost!
Anezka_8_4_421                  Anezka_8_4_421                  Therefore do I offer thee the amulet of Jude, patron saint of lost causes.
Christof_8_4_422                Christof_8_4_422                Oh...
Anezka_8_4_423                  Anezka_8_4_423                  Thou sweet heavens, guide him.
Ecat_9_1_432                    Ecat_9_1_432                    This is most excellent, Wilhem. Our alliance with the Cappadocians shall strengthen when I reveal Mercurio's treachery to Garinol. Wilhem, thou art a Brujah of surpassing splendor! Our Grandsire Brujah himself would feel pride at this.
Wilhem_9_1_433                  Wilhem_9_1_433                  In modesty, Mistress, I could not have done it without Christof's aid.
Cosmas_9_1_434                  Cosmas_9_1_434                  He has done well.
Wilhem_9_1_435                  Wilhem_9_1_435                  Christof performed admirably, my sire. I believe he is ready to know of the oncoming struggle.
Christof_9_1_436                Christof_9_1_436                What struggle is this?
Cosmas_9_1_437                  Cosmas_9_1_437                  The Jyhad. The great war of all the Vampire clans. The Tzimisce against the Tremere and us against both.
Christof_9_1_438                Christof_9_1_438                The Tremere?
Ecat_9_1_439                    Ecat_9_1_439                    The Tremere were once a house of mages, part of the Order of Hermes. They stole Caine's gift from an ancient of our kind, an Antediluvian, one of Caine's Grandchilder. They seek to infiltrate and control all the world. They couple the power of Caine with the ways of unspeakable magic.
Cosmas_9_1_440                  Cosmas_9_1_440                  The fiends from the Carpathians, the Tzimisce, are fighting the Tremere even as we speak. The Tzimisce have invaded the ancestral homelands of the Ventrue, which puts us in a strange alliance with the very Ventrue who destroyed our great city of Carthage. So we unite with the vampire prince of Prague, the Ventrue Rudolf Brandl- for now.
Wilhem_9_1_441                  Wilhem_9_1_441                  Thou hast reason to hate the Ventrue as well, Christof. Thou fell in battle against the pagan barbarians because the Ventrue made puppets of thy leaders. The Venture sent thee in battle to stop their Tzimisce enemies. Most of the barbarians were Revenants, ghoul servitors of the Tzimisce.
Cosmas_9_1_442                  Cosmas_9_1_442                  Other clans maintain havens here in Prague. Thou hast met the Cappadocians. The Nosferatu lurk below the graveyard in the northern quarter. Most of King Otakar's family, the Premysls, are Tzimisce ghouls. With the entrance of the Tremere into Prague, a struggle is bound to ensue, leaving the mortals greatly abused. We must avert this horror.
Ecat_9_1_443                    Ecat_9_1_443                    Many in Prague have disappeared, including Brujah. We suspect they fell victim to the Tzimisce Fiends or the Usurping Tremere. We must stop the abductions.
Wilhem_9_1_444                  Wilhem_9_1_444                  First we must seek to undo the damage done by the traitorous Mercurio. Mistress, I wish to approach the Jewish quarter and warn the Rabbi of Mercurio's meddling. If their Golem hath suffered a cowardly attack, it may not be able to protect the Jews from the Tzimisce.
Cosmas_9_1_445                  Cosmas_9_1_445                  We have no alliance with the Kabbalists in the Jewish quarter, and they have powerful charms of faith to wield 'gainst our kind.
Wilhem_9_1_446                  Wilhem_9_1_446                  We have a duty to protect the mortals.
Ecat_9_1_447                    Ecat_9_1_447                    Should we deliver unto them this news, mayhap the Jews will owe us a boon. But we have much to do first. In three night's time thou may deliver thy message. Take Christof with thee. But beware. The Kabbalists have no love for the likes of us.
Christof_9_1_449                Christof_9_1_449                Three night's time may be too late.
Christof_9_1_448                Christof_9_1_448                Very well.
Ecat_9_1_450                    Ecat_9_1_450                    After one mission with Wilhem, thou hast become very like him. So be it. Wilhem, take Christof to the Jewish quarter this night, and warn the Rabbi.
